.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt ICOWM-RANDR 5
.Os IcoWM 1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm randr.json
.Nd XRandR output profiles for IcoWM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa randr.json
describes what each physical display output should do: its resolution,
where it sits, how it is rotated, and whether it is used at all.
.Xr icowm 1
applies these through the XRandR extension at startup, and again
whenever an output is connected or disconnected.
.Pp
The file is optional.
Without it, hot-plug events are still handled and surfaces reflow as
outputs come and go; only the profiles are absent.
At most sixteen outputs are described, or two in a build compiled with
.Ic COMPACT ,
which is a compile-time option and not the restricted-memory mode
.Xr icowm-memguard 5
describes.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql false
Master switch for profile management.
While false the rest of the file is ignored.
.El
.Pp
.Ic outputs
holds an array, each entry describing one output.
An entry naming an output that is not currently connected is skipped
until one by that name appears, which is what makes a profile for a
monitor that is only sometimes plugged in useful.
Name matching is case-insensitive.
.Pp
The array is one single list shared by every X screen this session
manages, not one per screen.
On a multi-GPU setup where two screens each expose an output of the
same name, an entry naming it applies to both alike; there is no way
to profile one and leave the other alone.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic name Pq string
The connector name as the X server reports it, such as
.Ql HDMI-1 ,
.Ql eDP-1
or
.Ql DP-2 .
Run
.Xr xrandr 1
to see what is available.
.It Ic is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether this output is used.
True applies the rest of the profile and lets
.Xr icowm 1
place windows there.
False turns the output off outright, blanking it as though unplugged,
and excludes it from placement, which is what a permanently connected
output that should never receive windows wants.
.It Ic is-primary Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether this is the primary display.
Only applied when
.Ic is-enabled
is true, and as a separate step after the rest of the profile.
.It Ic resolution.w Pq integer, default Ql 0
.It Ic resolution.h Pq integer, default Ql 0
Mode to set, in pixels.
Zero leaves the output at whatever mode it already has.
.It Ic position.x Pq integer, default Ql 0
.It Ic position.y Pq integer, default Ql 0
Where the top left corner of this output sits in the combined screen.
.It Ic rotation Pq string, default Ql \&"normal\&"
One of
.Ql normal ,
.Ql left ,
.Ql right
or
.Ql inverted .
.El
.Ss Applying a change to a running session
.Pa randr.json
is re-read on every configuration reload and applied before anything
else that reload does, so that whatever resyncs afterward already sees
the new geometry.
.Pp
When the reload actually changes something, a dialog appears on the
affected screen asking whether to keep the new profile or go back to the
previous one, with
.Ql Revert
selected and a countdown running underneath.
Choosing
.Ql Keep
keeps it; choosing
.Ql Revert ,
pressing
.Ql Escape ,
or simply letting the countdown reach zero puts every changed output
back exactly as it was, mode, position, rotation and primary status
alike.
The countdown is ten seconds.
.Pp
This exists for the obvious reason: a profile that leaves a monitor
blank or unreadable would otherwise be very hard to undo, since undoing
it needs the very screen it just broke.
Letting it expire is therefore the safe outcome, which is why
.Ql Revert
is what is selected to begin with.
.Pp
No dialog appears when nothing actually changed, nor at startup, nor on
a hotplug event.
Only a reload offers it, that being the one moment somebody is at the
keyboard to have asked for it.
.Pp
On a machine with more than one screen, every screen still gets its
profiles applied on the same reload, but only the first one to actually
change is offered the dialog: only one can be open at a time, and only
the most recent change is remembered well enough to undo.
.Pp
Each profile is compared against the output's current state before
anything is written, so a file already matching reality issues no XRandR
request at all, including on a reload triggered by some unrelated file.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/icowm/randr.json
The file this page describes, at the first of the several locations
.Xr icowm 1
looks in.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A laptop panel with an external monitor to its right, the external one
primary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "is-enabled": true,
    "outputs": [
        {
            "name": "eDP-1",
            "is-enabled": true,
            "resolution": { "w": 1920, "h": 1080 },
            "position": { "x": 0, "y": 0 }
        },
        {
            "name": "HDMI-1",
            "is-enabled": true,
            "is-primary": true,
            "resolution": { "w": 2560, "h": 1440 },
            "position": { "x": 1920, "y": 0 }
        }
    ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A projector that stays plugged in but must never receive windows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "is-enabled": true,
    "outputs": [
        { "name": "DP-2", "is-enabled": false }
    ]
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
